<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="notes-week-04" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Week 4</title>

<introduction>
    <p>
        This is an outline of the topics we covered in Week 4, from September 21 to 25.
    </p>
</introduction>

<handout component="cur">
    <title>Monday 9/21</title>
    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Define increasing, decreasing, and constant functions.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Interpret piecewise functions, evaluate them at various points, and sketch graphs.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Interpret physical scenarios using functions.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>
    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Increasing and Decreasing Functions</title>

        <p>
            As we now start chapter 2, we want to expand what we can do with functions. For this first part, we want to tie graphical properties of a function to mathematical or algebraic properties.
        </p>

        <p component="b" workspace="3in">
            The first of these properties is <term>increasing</term>. What would it mean for a function to be increasing in terms of the graph?
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            The first of these properties is <term>increasing</term>. What would it mean for a function to be increasing in terms of the graph? It means that it goes further up as you move to the right. What does this mean in terms of points.
        </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </page>


    <page>
        <definition xml:id="def-inc-dec-fcns">
            <statement>
                <p component="b" workspace="4in">
                    Let <m>f</m> be a function defined on some interval <m>(a,b)</m>. We say that 
                </p>
                <p component="a">
                    Let <m>f</m> be a function defined on some interval <m>(a,b)</m>. We say that 
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                inc
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                dec
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                constant
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="3in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Consider the graph of the function <m>f(x)</m> drawn below. Determine intervals on which <m>f(x)</m> is increasing, decreasing, or constant.
                </p>
                <image width="60%">
  <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="fcns-inc-dec-diagram-one">
    <xi:include href="../../prefigure/fcns-inc-dec-diagram-one.xml" />
  </prefigure>
</image>
            </statement>
        </example>

        <p>
            In future classes, there will be a way to determine this in an analytical way from the function formula. However, for now, we're just looking at graphs.
        </p>
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Relative Maximum and Minimum</title>

            <p>
                Consider the graph of the function <m>f(x)</m> given below.
            </p>
            <image width="60%">
  <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="fcns-relmax-diagram-one">
    <xi:include href="../../prefigure/fcns-relmax-diagram-one.xml" />
  </prefigure>
</image>
<p>
    Are there are points that look like they are maxima or minima for this function?
</p>

<p component="b" workspace="4in">
    <nbsp />
</p>
<p component="a">
    Two ways to think about max and min. Everywhere, or nearby. 
</p>
            
        </paragraphs>
    </page>

<page>
    <definition xml:id="def-relmax-relmin">
        <statement>
            <p component="b" workspace="2in">
                Let <m>f(x)</m> be a function defined on some open interval around the point <m>c</m>. We say that  
            </p>
            <p component="a">
                Let <m>f(x)</m> be a function defined on some open interval around the point <m>c</m>. We say that <m>f(c)</m> is a relative maximum...
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
        The goal is to identify these things from a graph. We'll learn some tricks on getting exact values for some specific functions later this semester.
    </p>
</page>

